## Summary

Turbopack's `*_client-reference-manifest.js` files repeat the same chunk lists for every client reference: each `clientModules`, `ssrModuleMapping`, and `rscModuleMapping` entry of a page lists all the chunks of its chunk group, so a manifest is mostly copies of a handful of lists. On a large app the manifests totaled **1.67 GiB** on disk, were the single largest retained allocation site in the build (~1.7 GiB of the live heap at the end of `next build`), and took ~6 s to evaluate at runtime (`eval` in node).

This writes the manifest as a JS expression that evaluates to the same object, but:

- writes each distinct chunk path once, in a string table `s`, with directory prefixes that repeat shared as constants (`p0+"abc.js"`)
- writes each chunk list that is used more than once once, in a table `l`
- encodes lists as runs of consecutive string indices, `r(start,length,...)`, since paths are numbered in first-seen order
- builds entries with factories, `e(id,chunks)` / `n(id,chunks)`, instead of repeating `{"id":..,"name":"*","chunks":..,"async":false}`
- does the same for `entryJSFiles` and `entryCSSFiles` (inlined CSS contents go in the string table too, without prefix splitting)

